A function gives a reusable block of code a name.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    greet |name String| {
        io.line([: Hello, [name]])
    }
]

The function name comes first. The pipes enclose the parameter list. Here `name` gives the function a name for its String argument.

Use `||` when there are no parameters:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    say_hello || {
        io.line("Hello")
    }
]

Add an arrow when the function returns a value:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    double |value I32| -> I32 {
        return value * 2
    }
]

Write `-> !` when the function never returns:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    fatal |message String| -> ! {
        assert(false, message)
    }
]

That spelling is a whole return contract, not a type. See
@./ (Function declarations advanced) for the full rules.

A pair of braces encloses the statements in the function body.
